diff --git a/docs/OPENAUTO_CANCELLATION.md b/docs/OPENAUTO_CANCELLATION.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..989f188
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/OPENAUTO_CANCELLATION.md
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Android synthesis cancellation evaluation
+
+This local evaluation starts from upstream PocketTTS-Android-Engine 0.5.3,
+commit `37809d23d68ea17fdeb5faad5edc97771022cf9a`. It is not an upstream release.
+The functional correction is in `PocketTtsService.kt`, `NativePocketTts.kt` and
+`jni_bridge.cpp`. Model selection, model bytes and voice conditioning are unchanged.
+
+## Problem and correction
+
+The service previously returned from `onSynthesizeText` immediately after dispatching
+the work to its own executor. Android could therefore stop playback while native
+synthesis continued, or lose the stop target before playback had started. A new
+request then waited behind the old work. On the evaluation Pixel, that caused
+13–14 second restart delays during playback and a 47 second delay after early stop.
+
+The service now completes all synthesis work on Android's synthesis thread before
+returning. Each active request owns a cancellation flag and an optional reference
+to its native engine. Stop marks the request cancelled without waiting for model
+loading. Cancellation is checked after loading, before native stream creation,
+after stream publication, and before delivering PCM blocks. Cancelled requests do
+not emit a success or synthesis-failure callback from the service.
+
+The request handoff lock prevents a stop already targeting the old request from
+reaching the engine after that request has finished. It is not held across loading
+or inference. The native stream mutex protects publication, cancellation and removal
+of the stream pointer. The JNI handle remains alive while synthesis or stop uses it;
+close waits until those calls finish. Native stream teardown still waits for the
+current inference work to reach a cancellation point; this is not a hard real-time
+termination guarantee.
+
+Android's contract is documented in
+[TextToSpeechService](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/speech/tts/TextToSpeechService).
+
+## Isolated installation
+
+The debug application ID is `org.pockettts.android.engine.openautotest`, labelled
+`Pocket TTS (stop test)`. It coexists with the unmodified release engine. Both the
+OpenAuto AI assistant and its working DHU package remain unchanged. The test client
+selects the test engine explicitly; the system default TTS engine remains Google.
+
+The test build needs the same German FP32 model and licensed Alba reference as the
+baseline. These are separate assets, not bundled in the APK or source archive.
+For device automation, the known public assets are placed into the test app's own
+debug sandbox. This does not test the file-picker/import UI and does not read the
+release engine's private files.
+
+## Checks
+
+Build with JDK 17, the pinned Gradle wrapper, Android SDK 37, NDK 27.2.12479018 and
+CMake 3.22.1. Obtain ONNX Runtime 1.20.0 using the hashes in
+`scripts/prepare_android_native_deps.sh`, then run:
+
+```text
+./gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest :app:lintDebug :app:assembleDebug
+```
+
+The Windows test adjustment uses the JDK ZIP filesystem to represent Android's Unix
+paths, including colon-containing document IDs. It retains all security assertions.
+The CMake adjustment omits dr_libs' unused test submodules to avoid long Windows paths.
+
+The separate instrumentation client covers full offline output, stop during playback,
+immediate restart, process-cold early stop, rapid start/stop sequences, queue flush and
+client shutdown/reconnect. Results and exact APK hashes belong in the accompanying
+evaluation report; this document does not substitute for device results.
+
+No API requests, microphone captures, vehicle sessions, call tests or headset tests
+are involved. Callback latency is not acoustic latency. Listening confirmation is
+reported separately. This evaluation does not integrate Pocket into OpenAuto AI yet.
